from author import *
unit(6,'formulation','Formulate a failure-classification problem',
'''Machine learning estimates a mapping from observed features to a target using examples. Supervised learning has labels; unsupervised learning discovers structure without a predefined target. A classifier chooses a category, a regressor predicts a numeric value, and anomaly detection flags unusual observations without proving a defect.\n\nFor CI failures, define PRODUCT_DEFECT, AUTOMATION_DEFECT, ENVIRONMENT, TEST_DATA, and UNKNOWN. Label by evidence available to expert triage, record disagreements, and retain UNKNOWN instead of forcing false certainty. Define the prediction moment before choosing features.''',
'''Build one record per independent incident with log text, runtime context, label, timestamp, and group ID. Separate same-incident retries into the same split. Establish a majority-class or deterministic keyword baseline before a learned model.\n\nFeature extraction turns text and metadata into numbers: TF-IDF for log tokens, numeric duration, and one-hot environment categories. Fit preprocessing only on training examples in a Pipeline. Validate on later incidents or held-out groups to approximate real deployment.''',
'''A resolved defect ID or eventual owner is not available at failure time and leaks the answer. Randomly splitting identical stack traces gives near-duplicates to both train and test, making the benchmark unrealistically easy.\n\nInspect high-confidence errors and compare feature availability to the prediction timestamp. Find duplicate groups across splits. A useful classifier needs a review path for uncertain cases and a failure taxonomy that reflects meaningful routing decisions.''',
'''Report per-class precision, recall, support, and a confusion matrix; distinguish product-defect misses from harmless route mistakes. Compare with the baseline on the same held-out incidents and record latency and review burden.\n\nThe SDET owns dataset and labeling quality as much as model training. A report that says only model.fit succeeded does not answer whether the system can improve triage safely.''',
'from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nmodel=Pipeline([("tfidf",TfidfVectorizer()),("classifier",LogisticRegression(class_weight="balanced",max_iter=1000))])\n# model.fit(train_logs, train_labels); evaluate only on held-out incidents',
py('Implement disjoint_groups(train, test): return True only if incident-group identifiers do not overlap.','def disjoint_groups(train,test):\n    return set(train).isdisjoint(test)','assert disjoint_groups(["i1","i2"],["i3"])\nassert not disjoint_groups(["i1"],["i1","i2"])','def disjoint_groups(train,test):\n    return True'),
('Which feature is leakage for prediction at failure time?',['Current log text','Resolved defect owner','Current environment'],1,'A resolution produced after triage is unavailable at prediction time.'))
unit(6,'splits','Preprocessing, cross-validation, and generalization',
'''Training fits parameters, validation selects architecture and thresholds, and a final test set estimates the behavior of the selected system. Overfitting captures accidental training patterns; underfitting misses useful structure. Bias is systematic error from restrictions, while variance reflects sensitivity to the sample.\n\nCross-validation trains and validates repeatedly on different folds. It does not excuse leakage: time order, incident grouping, and transformation fitting still matter. A scaler or vocabulary fitted on all records exposes validation information.''',
'''Use GroupKFold for related incident records or a time-based split for changing releases. Put imputation, encoding, feature selection, and estimator in a Pipeline so each fold fits them only on its training partition.\n\nCompare learning curves: a persistent poor fit on both train and validation suggests insufficient representation or excessive regularization; a strong train score and weak validation score suggests overfit, shift, or leakage. Keep the final test set untouched until model selection is complete.''',
'''Scaling before the split and selecting features using test labels both invalidate the result. A benchmark can also fail without leakage when a new service produces vocabulary absent from training.\n\nBreak performance down by time, module, and environment. Compare model confidence with actual correctness, inspect mislabeled samples, and measure an abstention policy rather than hiding unknown categories.''',
'''Repeat model selection on reproducible folds and report mean and variation with the grouping strategy. Serialize the entire transformation pipeline and its vocabulary, not only model coefficients.\n\nThis is the ML version of test-environment parity: the code used to transform production input must match the code that generated the reported evaluation result.''',
'from sklearn.model_selection import GroupKFold\ncv=GroupKFold(n_splits=3)\n# for train_idx,val_idx in cv.split(X,y,groups=incident_ids):\n#     fold_model.fit(X[train_idx],y[train_idx])\n#     score=fold_model.score(X[val_idx],y[val_idx])',
rubric('A classifier scores 99% after TF-IDF was fit on all data and retries were split randomly. Repair the evaluation plan.',[{'label':'Grouped splitting','terms':['incident','group']},{'label':'Preprocessing boundary','terms':['pipeline','training']},{'label':'Independent final evidence','terms':['held-out','baseline']}], 'Group retries by incident, reserve a held-out time window, and fit TF-IDF plus classifier in a Pipeline within each training fold. Compare a baseline on matched held-out cases; report class-level errors and confidence calibration.'),
('Which operation belongs inside the training fold?',['Vocabulary fitting','Final test-set reporting','Business approval'],0,'A vocabulary learned from validation data is a form of leakage.'))
unit(6,'algorithms','Linear models, neighbors, trees, and ensembles',
'''Linear regression fits a numeric target by combining weighted features. Logistic regression applies a sigmoid or multiclass softmax to linear scores to model class probabilities; its name does not mean it predicts a continuous regression target. k-NN votes among nearby observations and depends strongly on representation and scaling.\n\nA decision tree repeatedly partitions features into regions and can express interactions, but a deep tree overfits. Random forests average many randomized trees to reduce variance. Gradient boosting adds learners that correct remaining errors, often requiring careful regularization and validation.''',
'''Choose a model based on feature type, dataset size, interpretability, and operational budget. Start with a regularized linear text model; compare tree ensembles on meaningful numeric/categorical features. Use standardized distances for k-NN and evaluate memory and query time because it retains training examples.\n\nTune depth, regularization, and ensemble size using validation rather than the final test set. For regression report MAE in original units, RMSE to emphasize large mistakes, and R² relative to a mean predictor; R² can be negative.''',
'''A tree can memorize a build ID that merely correlates with one incident. k-NN can call logs similar because of frequent boilerplate. An ensemble can improve average score while worsening the rare category engineers care about.\n\nInspect feature importance cautiously: importance does not establish causality, and correlated features divide or distort attribution. Run ablations and compare errors before choosing complexity.''',
'''Use one preprocessing and evaluation protocol across candidate models. Compare per-class tradeoffs, stability over releases, inference latency, artifact size, and analyst review time.\n\nA simple, calibrated baseline with a reliable UNKNOWN path is often preferable to a more accurate opaque model that routes serious defects confidently to the wrong owner.''',
'from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error,mean_squared_error,r2_score\ny=[2.,4.,6.]\np=[2.,3.,7.]\nprint(mean_absolute_error(y,p))\nprint(mean_squared_error(y,p)**.5)\nprint(r2_score(y,p))',
py('Implement mae(actual,predicted): equal non-empty lengths are required. Return mean absolute error.','def mae(actual,predicted):\n    if len(actual)!=len(predicted) or not actual: raise ValueError("invalid pair lengths")\n    return sum(abs(a-b) for a,b in zip(actual,predicted))/len(actual)','assert abs(mae([2,4,6],[2,3,7])-2/3)<1e-9\ntry: mae([],[])\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError("empty MAE is undefined")','def mae(actual,predicted):\n    return 0'),
('Which algorithm typically retains training instances for inference?',['k-NN','Logistic regression','A fitted decision tree'],0,'Neighbor prediction depends on stored examples and distance comparisons.'))
unit(6,'metrics','Confusion matrices, imbalance, and thresholds',
'''Accuracy can be high when a rare class is always missed. A confusion matrix counts actual and predicted categories. For a positive class, precision is TP/(TP+FP), recall is TP/(TP+FN), specificity is TN/(TN+FP), and F1 is the harmonic mean of precision and recall.\n\nROC plots true-positive versus false-positive rates as the threshold varies. PR curves focus on precision/recall and can be more informative when positives are rare. AUC summarizes ranking over thresholds and does not choose your deployed operating point.''',
'''Select the threshold on validation data using the cost of misses, false alarms, and human review. Use class weights or sampling carefully and keep evaluation at the natural population prevalence. Calibrate probabilities if decisions require meaningful confidence.\n\nFor multiclass triage, compute each class’s metrics and macro averages so frequent environment failures do not hide product-defect errors. A top probability below a validated threshold can route to UNKNOWN.''',
'''Oversampling before splitting copies examples into evaluation. Reporting only weighted F1 can conceal a rare-category failure. A confidence of 0.95 is not a 95% correctness guarantee without calibration evidence.\n\nTrace the chosen threshold to analyst capacity and missed-defect risk. Recheck its operating point after a prevalence change. Confidence intervals and support counts matter more than tiny decimal gains.''',
'''Hand-calculate a small confusion matrix, then compare the calculation with sklearn. Evaluate a threshold sweep on the same validation cases and record the resulting review workload.\n\nThis turns model selection into quality engineering: define a release gate that protects product-defect recall while placing a realistic ceiling on false alarms and review cost.''',
'tp,fp,fn,tn=18,6,2,74\nprecision=tp/(tp+fp)\nrecall=tp/(tp+fn)\nf1=2*precision*recall/(precision+recall)\nprint(precision,recall,f1)',
py('Implement prf(tp,fp,fn): return (precision, recall, f1). Use 0.0 for zero denominators.','def prf(tp,fp,fn):\n    p=tp/(tp+fp) if tp+fp else 0.0\n    r=tp/(tp+fn) if tp+fn else 0.0\n    return p,r,2*p*r/(p+r) if p+r else 0.0','p,r,f=prf(18,6,2)\nassert abs(p-.75)<1e-9 and abs(r-.9)<1e-9\nassert prf(0,0,0)==(0.,0.,0.)','def prf(tp,fp,fn):\n    return 0.,0.,0.'),
('Which metric directly measures missed actual product defects?',['Recall','Overall accuracy','MAE'],0,'Recall’s denominator contains actual positive cases, including false negatives.'),demo='threshold')
unit(6,'unsupervised','Clustering and anomaly detection for CI triage',
'''Clustering groups observations using a representation and distance rule; it does not assign trustworthy business labels. k-means approximates points with centroids and expects a chosen number of clusters. Density methods can identify noise, while anomaly models flag unusual patterns.\n\nAn unfamiliar error may be a new product defect, a valid new feature, or merely a changed logging format. Treat novelty as a review signal. The connection to supervised learning is representation: poor features produce misleading clusters and neighbors.''',
'''Normalize comparable vectors when cosine-like geometry is appropriate, inspect cluster samples, and join expert labels only for analysis. Compare clusters across time and modules. A distance threshold should be selected using known normal and anomalous cases, not guessed from a chart.\n\nBuild a baseline anomaly score from duration deviation and use it alongside log novelty. Keep environment and workload changes visible so a deployment does not trigger thousands of false alarms.''',
'''A cluster can reflect boilerplate or tenant names rather than failure mechanism. Outlier removal before analysis can delete precisely the incidents you wanted to find. Fitting an anomaly model on a dataset dominated by incidents teaches it that incidents are normal.\n\nInspect nearest neighbors and the feature contribution, then reproduce the condition. Measure how many flagged cases an engineer can meaningfully review and how quickly a novel defect is discovered.''',
'''Compare anomaly flags with an adjudicated slice and report review yield, time-to-detection, and missed known incidents. Retain a stable random sample of unflagged cases to estimate misses.\n\nAn SDET uses unsupervised tools to prioritize investigation, not to replace assertions. Your interview explanation should say what a distance score can and cannot establish.''',
'from statistics import mean,pstdev\ndurations=[2.,2.1,1.9,2.2]\nmu,sd=mean(durations),pstdev(durations)\nprint((5.-mu)/sd)',
py('Implement unusual(value,mean,sd,limit=3): flag an absolute z-score above limit. Reject sd <= 0.','def unusual(value,mean,sd,limit=3):\n    if sd<=0: raise ValueError("positive spread required")\n    return abs(value-mean)/sd > limit','assert unusual(10,2,1)\nassert not unusual(3,2,1)\ntry: unusual(2,2,0)\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError("undefined z score")','def unusual(value,mean,sd,limit=3):\n    return False'),
('An anomaly score establishes what?',['A proven product defect','Unusualness under the chosen representation','A correct owner'],1,'Novelty is evidence for investigation, not a causal diagnosis.'))
unit(7,'neuron','Neurons, activations, tensors, and initialization',
'''A neuron computes z=w·x+b and applies an activation. Without nonlinear activation, stacking linear layers still produces a linear transformation. Sigmoid maps to (0,1), tanh to (-1,1), ReLU uses max(0,z), and softmax turns a vector of logits into normalized positive probabilities.\n\nA tensor generalizes vectors and matrices to multiple axes. Label batch, feature, and class dimensions: a classification batch shaped (N,D) multiplied by weights (D,C) gives logits (N,C). Shape validity alone does not prove that axes mean the intended things.''',
'''Initialize weights with a scale appropriate to the activation rather than all zeros. Use ReLU in a small hidden layer and leave final logits unnormalized when a loss such as CrossEntropyLoss expects logits. Compute stable softmax by subtracting the maximum logit first.\n\nInspect a single sample before batching. Verify dtype, input shape, class indices, and output dimension. Keep training and evaluation modes explicit when dropout or normalization changes behavior.''',
'''Identical zero-initialized hidden weights preserve symmetry and cannot learn distinct features. Applying softmax before a loss that already incorporates log-softmax can weaken or distort optimization. A sigmoid with extreme logits saturates and gives tiny gradients.\n\nPrint intermediate shapes and compare a manual forward pass with the framework. Diagnose a wrong tensor axis before changing learning rate or adding layers.''',
'''Use the neuron playground to change x, weight, bias, and activation. Record when the output is zero, saturated, or linear. Test softmax sum and numerical stability on large logits.\n\nA failure classifier is only useful if input features and output class indices remain aligned across training, artifact serialization, and the API. This is a familiar contract-testing problem with numeric tensors.''',
'import math\ndef sigmoid(z):\n    return 1/(1+math.exp(-z)) if z>=0 else math.exp(z)/(1+math.exp(z))\nx,w,b=2.,.5,-.2\nprint(sigmoid(w*x+b))',
py('Implement relu_neuron(x,w,b): return max(0,w*x+b).','def relu_neuron(x,w,b):\n    return max(0,w*x+b)','assert relu_neuron(2,.5,-.2)==.8\nassert relu_neuron(-2,1,0)==0','def relu_neuron(x,w,b):\n    return 0'),
('Why do hidden layers need nonlinear activations?',['To make stacked transformations express nonlinear functions','To remove tensor axes','To avoid all validation'],0,'Composed linear transforms are still linear.'),demo='neuron',terms=['Neuron','ReLU','Softmax'])
unit(7,'gradient','Gradient descent and the learning-rate contract',
'''A derivative measures how a small input change affects a function. A gradient collects derivatives with respect to parameters. Gradient descent updates parameters opposite the gradient to reduce a differentiable loss locally. For L(w)=(w-3)², the derivative is 2(w-3).\n\nLearning rate controls step size. Too small can converge slowly; too large can overshoot or diverge. A lower training loss does not alone establish better generalization. The optimization objective and validation objective may differ.''',
'''Compute a forward prediction, scalar loss, gradients, and an update. In PyTorch, zero gradients before the next backward call because gradients accumulate by default. Separate the optimizer step from data iteration and log meaningful loss aggregates.\n\nAn epoch is a pass through training data; a minibatch is a subset used for one update. Shuffling improves stochastic training but keep deterministic seeds and independent evaluation splits for reproducibility.''',
'''Exploding loss can come from excessive learning rate, unscaled features, invalid labels, or an unstable numerical operation. Vanishing gradients can arise through saturated activations or long chains of multiplication.\n\nTest a tiny batch you should be able to overfit. If loss does not decrease there, inspect gradient values, parameter updates, labels, and loss input contract before blaming dataset scale.''',
'''Use the gradient visualizer to compare step sizes on the same quadratic objective. Finite differences approximate a derivative and provide a useful check of a manually derived gradient.\n\nIn production model training, store the learning-rate schedule, optimizer state, seeds, and validation curves. An engineer should explain both how optimization works and how they distinguish optimization failure from overfit.''',
'w=0.\nfor epoch in range(10):\n    gradient=2*(w-3)\n    w-=.1*gradient\nprint(w,(w-3)**2)',
py('Implement step(w,lr): take one gradient descent update for loss (w-3)^2.','def step(w,lr):\n    return w-lr*2*(w-3)','assert abs(step(0,.1)-.6)<1e-9\nassert step(3,.1)==3\nassert step(0,1)==6','def step(w,lr):\n    return w'),
('Why call optimizer.zero_grad() each ordinary batch?',['Gradients accumulate by default','It deletes the dataset','It normalizes labels'],0,'Unintended accumulation changes the optimization step.'),demo='gradient')
unit(7,'backprop','Backpropagation is the chain rule in a graph',
'''Backpropagation efficiently applies the chain rule through a computation graph. It does not search randomly for better weights. For h=w1*x, y=w2*h, and loss=(y-target)², propagate dL/dy=2(y-target), dL/dw2=(dL/dy)*h, and dL/dw1=(dL/dy)*w2*x.\n\nEvery local operation contributes its derivative. Reusing a parameter means its gradient receives contributions from every path. Automatic differentiation records these operations so a framework can perform the same calculation.''',
'''Compute one tiny network by hand, then compare gradients with PyTorch autograd and finite differences. Avoid modifying a tensor needed by backward in place. Detaching a tensor or converting through an external array can sever the computation graph.\n\nGradient checking uses a small epsilon: (L(w+epsilon)-L(w-epsilon))/(2*epsilon). Extremely small epsilon introduces floating-point noise; very large epsilon is a poor local approximation.''',
'''A layer’s parameters may have no gradients because the output was detached, the loss did not depend on them, or gradient tracking was disabled. A graph can also accidentally retain many past batches and grow memory.\n\nInspect requires_grad, grad_fn, and actual parameter gradients. Use no_grad for evaluation and loss.item for a detached logging scalar. Do not retain the training graph merely to print metrics.''',
'''The walkthrough displays forward values and the gradients flowing backward. Compare analytical and finite-difference results on a synthetic scalar network. Explain why changing an earlier weight changes the final loss through downstream operations.\n\nThis gives an SDET a principled way to diagnose broken training code rather than treating backward() as magic. Shape, dependency, and numeric contracts remain testable.''',
'x,w1,w2,target=2.,.5,3.,1.\nh=w1*x\ny=w2*h\ndy=2*(y-target)\nprint("dL/dw1",dy*w2*x,"dL/dw2",dy*h)',
py('Implement gradients(x,w1,w2,target): return (dL/dw1,dL/dw2) for the described network.','def gradients(x,w1,w2,target):\n    h=w1*x\n    dy=2*(w2*h-target)\n    return dy*w2*x,dy*h','assert gradients(2,.5,3,1)==(24.,4.)\nassert gradients(1,1,1,1)==(0.,0.)','def gradients(x,w1,w2,target):\n    return 0.,0.'),
('Converting a trainable tensor to a detached array and back can cause what?',['A broken gradient path','Automatic regularization','A larger context window'],0,'The new tensor does not preserve the original autograd dependency.'),demo='backprop')
unit(7,'training','Train and debug a small PyTorch classifier',
'''A multilayer perceptron combines dense layers and activations. Training minimizes a loss on labeled batches, while validation measures generalization on separate cases. Cross-entropy is appropriate for class logits; regression losses serve numeric targets.\n\nDropout randomly suppresses activations during training, weight decay penalizes parameter magnitude, and normalization stabilizes representations. These techniques do not repair leakage or labels that contradict one another.''',
'''Build a model with nn.Sequential, use integer class indices, and optimize CrossEntropyLoss with Adam. Call model.train for training and model.eval plus no_grad for validation. Track class support and early-stop based on a held-out criterion.\n\nCheckpoint model, optimizer, vocabulary or feature scaler, and class mapping together. Test that a saved model produces equivalent logits on a fixed fixture after loading.''',
'''Training loss decreases while validation loss rises when a model memorizes or shifts away from the held-out distribution. A constant prediction can indicate severe imbalance, label encoding errors, or an inappropriate output activation.\n\nCompare a linear baseline before blaming model size. Inspect errors per class, not just overall accuracy. Ensure class weights refer to the same category order as the label encoder.''',
'''The local PyTorch lab trains on fictional failures and reports confusion matrices and threshold-based UNKNOWN routing. The browser exercises teach the same numeric mechanisms without requiring a GPU.\n\nDocument whether training used CPU or GPU, seed behavior, versioned data, and nondeterministic operations. Reproducibility means recording limitations, not promising identical floating-point results on every device.''',
'import torch\nfrom torch import nn\nmodel=nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(8,16),nn.ReLU(),nn.Dropout(.1),nn.Linear(16,5))\nloss_fn=nn.CrossEntropyLoss()\noptimizer=torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(),lr=.001)\n# logits=model(batch_x); loss=loss_fn(logits,batch_y)\n# optimizer.zero_grad(); loss.backward(); optimizer.step()',
rubric('A five-class network outputs softmax twice, sees validation loss rise, and cannot be reproduced after saving. Diagnose and repair all three boundaries.',[{'label':'Loss contract','terms':['logits','cross-entropy']},{'label':'Generalization','terms':['validation','early']},{'label':'Artifact parity','terms':['mapping','scaler']}], 'Feed logits to cross-entropy. Inspect overfit and leakage, compare the linear baseline, regularize and early-stop on held-out validation. Save the class mapping, scaler, model, optimizer state, seed, and dataset version; verify reload parity.'),
('When should dropout be active?',['During training, disabled in evaluation mode','Always including validation','Only when saving'],0,'Evaluation should use the learned network without stochastic dropout.'))
unit(7,'architectures','From CNNs and recurrent networks to attention',
'''A CNN applies shared filters across local neighborhoods, learning patterns independent of their exact position. Pooling trades spatial detail for invariance. For logs, a one-dimensional convolution can detect local token patterns; image applications use spatial dimensions.\n\nAn RNN updates a hidden state sequentially, sharing parameters across positions. Long chains can struggle with gradients and long-range information. LSTM gates control what enters, persists, and leaves a memory state, reducing some of those difficulties.''',
'''Choose architecture by structure: MLP for tabular features, CNN for local patterns, RNN/LSTM for sequences when sequential state is useful. Mask padding and distinguish batch, sequence, and feature axes.\n\nAttention can directly combine information from distant positions rather than forcing every dependency through one recurrent state. Transformers make this combination highly parallelizable during training, though autoregressive generation still produces dependent next tokens.''',
'''A model may learn padding artifacts or sequence length instead of failure content. Recurrent hidden state accidentally reused across independent incidents leaks context. Flattening all tokens into an MLP loses positional relationships unless explicitly represented.\n\nTest invariants by changing padding, permuting irrelevant boilerplate, and separating incidents. Measure meaningful improvements against simpler representations before choosing an architecture for novelty alone.''',
'''Explain the evolution from local filters and recurrent memory to content-addressed attention. Use the next phase’s attention calculation to see what direct cross-token interaction means.\n\nFor AI quality engineering, the main objective is diagnosing representation and state failures, not memorizing architecture names. A technical interview answer should connect computational structure to its tradeoffs.''',
'def moving_pattern(sequence):\n    return [sequence[i]+sequence[i+1] for i in range(len(sequence)-1)]\nprint(moving_pattern([1,0,1,1]))',
choose('Which architecture uses a hidden state updated sequentially across tokens?',['MLP','RNN/LSTM','A SQL join'],'RNN/LSTM'),
('What attention helps address relative to a single recurrent state?',['Direct access to distant token information','Elimination of all inference latency','Guaranteed truthful outputs'],0,'Attention combines selected information from positions without one recurrent bottleneck.'))
